A||||8||Greg Piet||Fan Switch
Z||000000||Greg Piet||08-31-99||11:49 PM||||I was looking through teh Fiero Stoer catalog, and although it s only 16 bucks for a 160 deg thermostat, they was $45 for the fan switch.<br>Call me carzy but that seems REALLY EXPENSIVE FOR A SWITCH.  Are there other options/sources available?
Z||000001||Greg Piet||08-31-99||11:53 PM||||Darn I really need to proofread these things before I press the submit button.  Sorry all!
Z||000002||Patrick||09-01-99||01:50 AM||||<br>Well, I paid about $2 for a nice switch similar in shape to the Fiero's headlight switch (but much smaller) and then used about $.20 worth of wire and installed a manually operated fan control on my dash. <p>It works great. When I'm in stop and go traffic in the summer, I turn the fan on. Any other time I leave it off. The car has NEVER overheated. Of course, I live up north in the frozen tundra of Canada.<IMG SRC="/ubb/biggrin.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p><p>
Z||000003||ChadMan||09-01-99||07:40 AM||||Hey, Patrick, which wire did you tap into when you wired your fan switch?  I was thinking of doing the same thing and saw on the schematic that there's a green wire coming from the back of the climate controls that seems to do nothing but turn the fan on when the Max or Norm A/C button is depressed.  That would seem to be a good candidate.
Z||000004||Greg Piet||09-01-99||11:14 AM||||I dont know what the wire color is, but just by grounding the switch wire the fan kicks on.<br>Yes that is always an option, but I was looking for possibly another source for a low temp switch
Z||000005||Patrick||09-01-99||11:38 PM||||<br>ChadMan, I hate to sound stupid, but it was over two years ago when I installed the switch and I sorta forget how I did it. You have to keep in mind that there were a whole lotta things that I had to fix or change on this car when I got it. The other thing to keep in mind is I believe that there are variations in how the fan is controlled in different models of the Fiero. <p>Hang on, it's coming back to me now..... I think I ran a wire from the ground of the <b>fan relay</b> which is located at the front on the drivers side. The switch I installed simply grounds the relay. So I <b>didn't</b> splice into the existing relay ground wire to make or break the connection, but I added <b>another</b> path to ground which I control with the switch. Does that make any sense?<p><p>
Z||000006||Groot||09-02-99||05:32 AM||||Hi,<p>I did the same with my 84 2.5L<br>Do you have a two-speed-fan ?<br>it's either the light-green-white or<br>dark-green-white that has to be connected<br>to ground. This is indeed from the relay<br>at the left-front-side<p>Succes,<br>Egbert
Z||000007||Nick||09-02-99||05:41 AM||||I've got an alternative for you.  Go here:<p>http://store.summitracing.com/product.asp?ssid=61VJHCXVN2VWRY11IBX1S7PF4L&p=A6ED197F-3612-11D3-949D-080009FC5B5F<p>You'll probably have to cut and paste that.  Sorry!<p>Click on the adjustable temp fan control.  It's only $23.95 and it works great.  I had it on my old 86 GT, with a 180 thermostat, and the temp stayed between 180 and 195 at all times, no matter how long it sat and idled.  The switch is adjustable, so if you wanted it to run cooler or hotter than that, you can just turn the knob.  <p>It has a sensor that you mount in the hose right next to the thermostat, which senses the temp. of the coolant as it comes out of the engine.  Since I have a 180 deg. stat, it would be pointless to set the switch lower than that.  My stat opens up, and when that coolant gets hot enough, the fan kicks on and cools off the engine, then shuts off again.  The thermostat is more than enough to keep the car cool when going down the road, but the switch makes sure the car doesn't get too hot when you are sitting in traffic not moving.<p>I don't know if the above would be the same for a 4-cyl. or not.  <p>For the wiring, it's pretty simple.  You locate your stock fan switch sending unit.  It should have a green wire, and splice into it.  Don't cut it though, because you still want it to function correctly in case the aftermarket switch fails.  You run that wire to the #1 terminal on the switch, and run the #3 terminal to ground.  That's it.  Now to set it properly, turn the knob all the way clockwise (highest setting), let the car get up to temp., the thermostat will open up, and when it gets to the temp. that you want the fan to turn on, slowly turn the knob counter-clockwise until the fan kicks on.<p>There are instructions that come with it with more details, but that should do it.  If you have any questions, ask away. <p><p>
Z||000008||Patrick||09-02-99||05:10 PM||||<br>I had a look today at my wiring and the best I can determine is that I tapped into the green and white wire coming from the fan relay. I think it's light green, but it's hard to tell. The headlamp assembly is in the way. <p>Oh, by the way, did I mention that the driver's side headlamp assembly has to come out to gain access to the fan relay?<IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p>Egbert, if it was me you were asking, I have the single speed fan.<p><br>
